\begin{abstract}
This manuscript presents the mathematical foundations, numerical architecture, and computational performance of the SIE-ASTRO v4.0 relativistic electromagnetic particle engine. 
The system integrates a fully parallelized relativistic Boris integrator, a constrained-transport Maxwell solver on a staggered Yee mesh, and an AES-256-GCM cryptographic ingestion pipeline. 
The implementation is written in C++20 and leverages OpenMP for shared-memory parallelism and OpenSSL for authenticated encryption. 
The goal of this work is to provide a rigorous academic exposition of the numerical methods and software design principles underlying the SIE-ASTRO v4.0 framework.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Relativistic plasma simulation requires numerical schemes capable of evolving charged particles under electromagnetic fields while preserving divergence constraints and ensuring long-term stability. 
The SIE-ASTRO v4.0 engine is designed as a production-grade computational framework for such simulations, integrating modern C++20 features with high-performance parallelism.

Two representative excerpts from the implementation highlight the core numerical components:
\begin{quote}
``Parallel Relativistic Boris Integrator'' \\
``Constrained Transport Magnetic Field Advancement''
\end{quote}

This manuscript formalizes the mathematical and computational structure of these components.

\section{Relativistic Particle Dynamics}
The motion of a charged particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ in electromagnetic fields is governed by the relativistic Lorentz force:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\bm{p}}{dt} = q\left( \bm{E} + \bm{v} \times \bm{B} \right),
\end{equation}
where the momentum is $\bm{p} = \gamma m \bm{v}$ and the Lorentz factor is
\begin{equation}
\gamma = \left( 1 - \frac{|\bm{v}|^2}{c^2} \right)^{-1/2}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Relativistic Boris Integrator}
The Boris method is widely used in particle-in-cell (PIC) simulations due to its excellent stability properties. 
The relativistic extension used in SIE-ASTRO v4.0 follows the structure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Half-step electric acceleration:
    \begin{equation}
    \bm{u}^{-} = \gamma\bm{v} + \frac{q\Delta t}{2m}\bm{E}.
    \end{equation}
    \item Magnetic rotation:
    \begin{equation}
    \bm{u}' = \bm{u}^{-} + \bm{u}^{-} \times \bm{t},
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}
    \bm{t} = \frac{q\Delta t}{2m\gamma'}\bm{B}, \qquad
    \bm{s} = \frac{2\bm{t}}{1 + |\bm{t}|^2}.
    \end{equation}
    \item Final half-step electric acceleration:
    \begin{equation}
    \bm{u}^{+} = \bm{u}' + \bm{u}' \times \bm{s}.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

The updated velocity is:
\begin{equation}
\bm{v}_{n+1} = \frac{\bm{u}^{+}}{\gamma_{\text{new}}}.
\end{equation}

\section{Maxwell Solver via Constrained Transport}
The electromagnetic fields evolve according to Maxwell's equations. 
SIE-ASTRO employs a staggered Yee mesh and a constrained-transport (CT) update to preserve the divergence-free condition:
\begin{equation}
\nabla\cdot\bm{B} = 0.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Discrete Faraday Update}
The CT update for the magnetic field is:
\begin{align}
B_x^{n+1} &= B_x^n - \Delta t\left(
\frac{E_z(i,j+1,k) - E_z(i,j,k)}{\Delta y}
-
\frac{E_y(i,j,k+1) - E_y(i,j,k)}{\Delta z}
\right), \\
B_y^{n+1} &= B_y^n - \Delta t\left(
\frac{E_x(i,j,k+1) - E_x(i,j,k)}{\Delta z}
-
\frac{E_z(i+1,j,k) - E_z(i,j,k)}{\Delta x}
\right), \\
B_z^{n+1} &= B_z^n - \Delta t\left(
\frac{E_y(i+1,j,k) - E_y(i,j,k)}{\Delta x}
-
\frac{E_x(i,j+1,k) - E_x(i,j,k)}{\Delta y}
\right).
\end{align}

This update preserves $\nabla\cdot\bm{B}$ to machine precision.

\section{Software Architecture}
The SIE-ASTRO v4.0 framework is organized into three core modules:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \texttt{Particles.hpp}: Relativistic particle system and Boris integrator.
    \item \texttt{GridFields.hpp}: Yee grid and constrained-transport Maxwell solver.
    \item \texttt{CryptoPipeline.hpp}: AES-256-GCM encryption pipeline.
\end{enumerate}

The main execution loop performs:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Field update,
    \item Particle push,
    \item Diagnostic encryption every 250 steps.
\end{enumerate}

\section{AES-256-GCM Secure Diagnostics}
Diagnostic frames are encrypted using AES-256-GCM with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 32-byte key,
    \item 12-byte IV,
    \item 16-byte authentication tag.
\end{itemize}

Representative implementation excerpts include:
\begin{quote}
``EVP\_EncryptInit\_ex cipher initialization failed.'' \\
